> I'm not sure where you're starting from for that rule--it's not in
> Grep's original query, and it's not Tolkien (Gandalf the Grey/White
> definitely used magic to kill).

It is the standard definition. Magic is "white" if it does not harm
anyone (divination, healing, and corresponding stuff), and "black" if
it harms someone (curses, diseases, storms, natural catastrophes, and
like stuff). 

Others mentioned Wiccans already on this thread, but this division is
actually much older than Wicca. The Oxford English Dictionary quotes
Chaucer as the earliest English author making the distinction, though
he used "natural magic" instead of "white magic". The first explicit
mention of "white magic" is from 1720.

For a long time this division was also honored in witch trials: in the
Middle Ages only those witches who had practiced black magic were
tried. The all-inclusive ban of magic came only later when the source
of all magic was attributed to devil.

